WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.130
As the underpinnings of
some democratic countries

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.853
are being called into question,

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.930
China is holding out its model
as a thriving alternative.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.550
Over the past four decades,

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.910
Beijing has increasingly
asserted its power

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.400
on the global stage, and is now on course

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.540
to become the world's biggest economy

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.243
two years sooner than
predicted pre-pandemic.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.600
For China, this success
legitimizes its model

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.320
as an alternative to the
liberal values of the West.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.120
The world is undergoing
changes unseen in a century,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.740
and now is the time for major development

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.273
and major transformation.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.260
But for the U.S. and its allies,

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.920
this authoritarian blueprint
is seen as a threat

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.713
to their democratic ideals.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.150
Democracy doesn't happen by accident.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.523
We have to defend it, fight for
it, strengthen it, renew it.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.890
We have to prove that our
model isn't a relic of history.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.700
We used to think that the
U.S./China competition

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.900
was mainly about economic leadership

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.100
or mainly about military
primacy in the western Pacific.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.080
Clearly it's a competition
of systems as well,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.420
and whether those systems
can perform adequately

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.763
in the 21st century.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.330
And the global pandemic
and its aftershocks

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.850
have only heightened tensions

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.170
between these competing systems.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.730
This is the first global crisis

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:30.950
in which American failed to lead the West.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.220
When historians come back to
look at the issue of COVID

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.820
and geopolitics, they
will ask was 2020/21,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.520
were those pivotal years?

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.290
Was that the year when
suddenly Asian countries,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.500
China in particular, really began to seem

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.900
as if they were back on top?

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.737
So what does it mean for the world

00:01:49.737 --> 00:01:52.150
as two superpowers go head to head,

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:55.293
not just over trade or
borders, but ideology?

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.510
Three hours, four minutes,
32 seconds and counting,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.120
right on time as far as
the astronaut countdown-

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.500
In the 1960s, while the U.S. was putting

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.130
a man on the moon, China
was struggling to feed

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.710
its one billion people
during the decade-long

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:19.882
Cultural Revolution.

00:02:19.882 --> 00:02:23.170
In the 1960s, both
countries were going through

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.500
periods of social upheaval.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.860
The U.S. was dealing with
the racial reckoning,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.040
the civil rights movement.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.850
But at the same time, there was

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.911
a favorable economic environment.

00:02:34.911 --> 00:02:38.860
China, on the other hand, had
a period of social upheaval

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.270
where the economy was
completely stagnant and ruined

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.170
by collectivist agricultural
policies in particular

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.430
that caused widespread famine.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.090
A lot of the educated people in Beijing

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.940
were taken out to the countryside.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.110
There were purges all
throughout the party.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.840
All had a big cost on the economy.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.047
After the death of Mao Zedong in 1976,

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:03.550
it was the reforms of Deng Xiaoping

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.480
that led China to
prioritize economic growth

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.550
even if it meant going against
core communist ideology.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:14.290
Deng was one of China's most
influential leaders ever,

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.750
and basically responsible for
the China that you see today.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.300
This was a night that was
going to be different.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.970
During the Cultural Revolution,
he was shunted aside

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.980
by Mao himself, and
then later brought back

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.120
and rehabilitated.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.267
And he went on to really
spark the reforms,

00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:33.410
the reform and opening up period

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:37.020
that led to the economic miracle
that we've seen in China.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.160
Delighted in putting it
on for the photographers

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.520
and waved enthusiastically to the crowd.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.300
He brought China back on the world stage

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:47.700
and put it out in front
of global investors

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.973
for the first time, really,
since the Cultural Revolution.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.840
The country opened up to private business

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.440
and foreign investment.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.050
Dominant state-owned banks
turned savings into financing

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.700
for roads, rail links and
ports, and foreign firms

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.460
were forced to set up
alongside a local partner,

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.653
encouraging the transfer of technology.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.710
As a result, the economy grew 90-fold

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:17.240
over the next four decades.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.500
Deng Xiaoping never had
an absolute blueprint

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.120
about what China would look like

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.240
at the end of the reform process.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.580
It was very experimental,
so certain reforms

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.150
were introduced in
particular localities first

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.250
to see if they would work,
and once they seemed to work,

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.250
then additional localities were allowed

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:38.640
to experiment with it.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.710
So that's the case of
special economic zones

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.543
is a good example of that.

00:04:42.543 --> 00:04:44.320
It started out with four
special economic zones

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.280
that allowed foreign direct investment in,

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.320
and then later on coastal cities,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.100
and now pretty much all of China.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.050
So that experimental sense

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:55.630
and almost improvisational
approach towards governance

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.063
is a hallmark of reform era China.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.240
For many outside China, it was expected

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.210
that as the country's economy
became more capitalist,

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:08.210
its politics would become more democratic.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.860
Other countries in Asia,
South Korea, for example,

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.670
were dictatorships at one point.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.580
They opened up their
economy, they became known

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.740
as Asian Tigers, and democracy
sort of came with that.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.080
So many people thought the more
China exports to the world,

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.980
the more it'll open up politically

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.870
and kind of follow the path

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:31.420
that some of those other
countries in Asia had taken.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.080
That wasn't the case, however.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.053
You know, China maintained
political control.

00:05:37.263 --> 00:05:39.460
And this control was maintained

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.640
by using a blueprint
known as The China Model,

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:44.810
a blend of state-directed capitalism

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.980
and political authoritarianism

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.840
that consists of four key elements

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.580
to keep the one-party state thriving:

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.610
focusing on the economy,
stoking national pride,

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.110
keeping party discipline,
and allowing no challengers

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.930
who may be critical of the government.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.418
All day, troops have
patrolled the streets,

00:06:04.418 --> 00:06:07.760
firing indiscriminately to
keep people off the streets.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.190
Tiananmen was a part of that,

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:13.700
the fact that they used deadly force

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.140
to stop demonstrations in their tracks.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:20.650
They also cracked down on free speech.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:24.960
They were able to do that
by delivering for the people

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.710
in a way that the masses
were seeing economic gains.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:32.640
They were seeing real
improvements in their livelihood,

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.870
and that has underpinned the legitimacy

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:38.860
of the Communist Party even
as it's really eliminated

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:42.680
all sort of political
dissent, both from without

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.663
and within the party over the years.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.630
Beijing also argues its model removes

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.230
the short-term demands
of an election cycle,

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:53.300
creating the stability needed to plan

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.393
and execute long-term strategies.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.180
But in the more than
seven decades that the CCP

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.670
has been in power, experts
do point to an evolution

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.903
in the Chinese authoritarian system.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.520
I think it would be
misleading to talk about

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.070
a monolithic China model because so much

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:13.780
has changed over different phases

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.550
of the People's Republic
of China's history.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.650
Many social scientists have
come up with various terms

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.130
to modify it, to qualify the nature

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.960
of authoritarianism in China, so it may be

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.500
consultative authoritarianism,
fragmented authoritarianism,

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.890
responsive authoritarianism,
decentralized authoritarianism.

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.260
What all of these adjectives get at

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.800
are the different spaces that exist

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.020
in China's political
system for adaptation,

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:45.430
for responsiveness, for even participation

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:49.293
and even policy influence that's
embedded within the system.

00:07:51.497 --> 00:07:54.420
At the turn of the 21st
century, both the Chinese

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:57.140
and U.S. models appeared
to be flourishing,

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:00.160
but then the 2008 financial crisis hit.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.140
Lehman Brothers, a 158-year-old firm,

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.770
filed for bankruptcy.

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.570
Stoking a rivalry between the superpowers

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.550
and their models of government.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.790
The 2008 financial crisis
seemed to call into question

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:15.650
multiple aspects of the
international system at once.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:17.800
It certainly called into question

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:20.560
the allure of the U.S. economic model,

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.380
which was seen to have caused
the crisis in the first place.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:28.180
It raised the possibility
that authoritarian capitalism

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:31.500
might out-perform liberal
democratic capitalism

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:32.800
over the long run.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:34.410
And then not least of all,

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:36.410
it convinced many Chinese officials

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:38.500
that the United States
was in geo-political

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.170
and geo-economic decline,
and so if you look back

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:45.200
at the critical inflection
point in a variety

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:48.410
of Chinese efforts to revise the terms

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:49.650
of the international system here,

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.470
simply being more open about the idea

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.260
that China might
eventually seek to displace

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.350
the United States, you
can trace those back

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:58.933
to the aftermath of the
global financial crisis.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:03.410
And now a new global crisis has thrown

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.140
The China Model back onto the spotlight,

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:08.970
and initially it didn't look so good.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.450
The silencing of Dr. Li Wenliang,

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.210
the whistleblower who tried
to warn China's authorities

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:16.600
about a deadly coronavirus outbreak

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.223
confirmed the opacity of Beijing's system.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:24.230
The model itself leads
to a lack of transparency

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:27.120
that ultimately raises questions

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.243
about China's response to the virus.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.840
Opening up the books and
showing everybody what happened

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:35.290
is just not in the DNA of
the Chinese authorities

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.030
to kind of reveal what happened

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.240
because possibly it could
show something much worse,

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.110
that they screwed up, and that would be

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:43.290
devastating for them politically,

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:45.700
which is the ultimate
weakness of The China Model

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:48.580
in the sense that everything about it

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.493
is geared toward keeping the
Communist Party in power.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.630
But that same China model
also allowed the government

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:58.920
to enforce the first large-scale
lockdown in modern times,

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:01.430
and quickly saw a mass social compliance

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.840
that helped to contain the virus.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.870
I mean, that's remarkable
infrastructural power,

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:09.040
but that wasn't just because
the government's powerful,

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.660
it's because rank and file health workers,

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.210
public security people,
transportation people,

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.560
they were all on board, participating,

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:22.670
in enforcing those lockdowns
and limiting mobility.

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:24.650
So from the government's perspective,

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:27.780
that's an example of
we're all in it together,

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:29.550
we all share the same goal.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.540
It's actually extremely
expensive and de-legitimizing

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:37.010
to just rely on brute force to
enforce a particular policy,

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:40.580
so you actually need
cooperation by societal actors

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:44.240
to provide that reality
check and also buy in,

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:45.900
that they're part of the process,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:49.710
that the government actually
cares about their concerns

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:53.513
and will be responsive
when they bring up issues.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.810
Although doubt surrounds
the accuracy of numbers

00:10:56.810 --> 00:11:00.140
reported by China, it was
inevitable that comparisons

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.860
would be drawn with its rival superpower.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:05.140
I think there's a big false positive,

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:07.940
that people look at the
current situation with COVID

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.560
and they draw the conclusion

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:13.670
that because the world's
leading totalitarian country

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:16.625
has done better, generally,
at fighting COVID

00:11:16.625 --> 00:11:18.860
than the world's leading
democracy, they draw the conclusion

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:21.480
that democracy is not good
at dealing with things

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.130
like COVID, and that is patently rubbish.

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:25.830
In general, if you're in an autocracy,

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.450
you're in a worse situation.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:29.690
Yes, okay, China did okay,
but you wouldn't have wanted

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.500
to be in Iran during this,

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.310
you wouldn't have wanted
to be in any of the Stans,

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:34.660
you wouldn't have wanted
to be in North Korea,

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.300
you wouldn't want to be in Russia.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:39.010
So on the whole, the
countries that have done well,

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:41.240
Australia, New Zealand, Singapore, Japan,

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:44.020
South Korea, these are all democracies.

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:47.117
The levels of difference are so colossal

00:11:47.117 --> 00:11:49.660
between the countries that did this well

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:51.270
and the ones that did it badly,

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.660
that the biggest answer in
all this is whether you had

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:55.860
governments that worked
and knew what to do.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.150
It was the notable absence
of the U.S. government

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:03.030
under President Donald
Trump to lead a response

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.550
during the early stages of the pandemic,

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.800
and the subsequent catastrophic
death toll in America

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.900
that caused many to question
the strength of its model,

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:13.820
including Beijing.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:16.850
In some ways, the COVID
pandemic is a replay

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.330
of the global financial
crisis in the effect

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:21.660
that it has had on Chinese behavior

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:23.020
in the near term at least.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:26.360
And so the global financial
crisis convinced Chinese leaders

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:28.610
that they had a window in
which they could behave

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:30.960
more assertively in areas
from the South China Sea

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:32.730
to the East China Sea and beyond

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.100
and reap new geo-political gains.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:38.373
COVID has clearly had the same
impact on Chinese leaders.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.980
This assertiveness under Xi Jinping

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.020
means doubling down on border disputes

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:47.170
in the South China Seas and
along the border with India,

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:49.320
while also looking to consolidate power

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:51.370
in Tibet and Hong Kong.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.440
Xi Jinping has really emphasized this,

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.945
is to overcome what they call
the century of humiliation

00:12:56.945 --> 00:12:59.897
in China, where a bunch of
foreign powers basically

00:12:59.897 --> 00:13:03.580
ran over China forced them
to sign trade agreements

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:06.820
that were exploitive,
and this really drives

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:09.460
the Chinese political environment.

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:11.900
Everything that's done cannot be seen

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:13.440
to be bowing to the West.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.070
China needs to assume its
rightful place on the world stage.

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:20.490
And you see China now on a global stage

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.730
challenging the U.S. leadership role

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:25.090
and the role of the West more generally,

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.960
in trying to tell China
how to run its government,

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:31.213
how to run its country and
how to run its economy.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.440
During certain periods of Chinese history,

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:47.660
many of its policies,
both domestic and foreign,

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:50.780
were indeed driven by
ideology because after all,

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:53.710
the People's Republic of China
was founded on communism.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:57.180
But now it's not as if today's
Chinese Communist Party

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:00.070
is interested in exporting socialism

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:02.950
or fomenting communist
revolutions around the world.

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:07.430
Their policies actually are
not ideologically driven.

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:09.300
I think a lot of their behavior, however,

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:13.760
is nationalistically driven
to protect China's interests,

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:17.237
so I think if you asked people in China,

00:14:17.237 --> 00:14:20.510
is there an ideological
conflict, they might say

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:23.693
well yes, the ideology is
actually coming from the West.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:28.700
As this ideological rivalry increases,

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:32.123
it's spilling over into many
aspects of the global economy.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.870
Why is technology such a key flashpoint

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:38.960
in the U.S./China relationship?

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:40.400
In part, is has to do with the fact

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.260
that technological innovation in areas

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.810
like artificial intelligence will shape

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:48.110
the balance of economic and
military power in this century,

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.440
but it's also because those technologies

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:54.440
have massive implications
for the ideological struggle

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.730
between authoritarianism and democracy.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.960
China is, for instance, trying to develop

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:01.360
what can best be described
as a high tech police state

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:04.430
at home that uses a
combination of big data,

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:06.950
artificial intelligence
and other technologies

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:11.203
to monitor and control the
behavior of the population.

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:15.010
Technology is just one
of an increasing number

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:16.380
of flashpoints.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.730
As China accuses the U.S. of stoking

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:21.170
Hong Kong's pro-democracy protests,

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:23.170
Washington condemns the mass detention

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:24.713
of Uyghurs in Xinjiang.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:28.890
The Biden administration
is also taking action

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.870
to protect its model,
such as increased spending

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:34.270
for manufacturing self-sufficiency

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:36.213
and strengthening ties with Taiwan.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:39.900
We are in the midst of
a fundamental debate

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:43.070
about the future and
direction of our world.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:46.800
That autocracy is the best
way forward, they argue,

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:50.083
and those who understand
that democracy is essential,

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:53.460
essential to meeting these challenges,

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:56.700
and I believe that
every ounce of my being,

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:59.373
that democracy will and must prevail.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.690
This isn't the first time that Americans

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:05.520
and citizens in other democratic societies

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.070
have worried that autocratic models

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:09.610
will be the wave of the future.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:13.620
We saw something very similar
during the Great Depression,

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:15.950
we saw something very
similar in parts of the world

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:18.440
after World War II.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:21.980
What we realize in retrospect
is that the weaknesses

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:25.600
of democracies always look
more pronounced in the moment,

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.500
because those weaknesses are
out there for everyone to see,

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.150
whereas autocratic regimes like China's

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:34.910
tend to assiduously
conceal their own failures

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.163
and their own weaknesses.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.260
And with potential financial

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:41.410
and demographic challenges looming,

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:43.830
there may be other threats
to the sustainability

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:47.053
of the China model that could
arise from within the country.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:52.190
The China Model is you
can grow the economy

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:55.320
while maintaining a one-party rule.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:57.970
Yet at the same time,
there's real questions

00:16:57.970 --> 00:17:01.070
about how long you can continue this

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:03.180
as your population gets wealthier,

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:06.800
as it gets more educated,
as it gets more integrated

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:10.330
with the world and has
ideas from other places,

00:17:10.330 --> 00:17:13.913
and has demands for better
livelihoods and more say.

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:19.250
At the same time, how does Xi
Jinping transfer power one day

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:20.613
and when does he step down?

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:23.870
The sustainability of the China model

00:17:23.870 --> 00:17:25.570
is an open question at this point.

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:31.830
No two leaves in the world are identical

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:36.173
and no histories, cultures or
social systems are the same.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:41.150
Each country is unique, with
its own history, culture

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:45.393
and social system, and none
is superior to the other.

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:49.100
I think the lessons, if you
look back through history,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:49.933
are really clear.

00:17:49.933 --> 00:17:52.900
Plagues are things that either cause

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:56.260
the downfalls of particular
orders or cause a re-think.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:18:00.020
So when you have these
momentary disasters,

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:03.130
that's the moment when you do wake up.

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:06.410
By any measure, you look at
the democracies of this world,

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:08.630
you look at Europe and America together,

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:10.340
and they are stronger than China.

00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:13.280
If you have a West that begins
to reform its governments,

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:16.840
that begins to talk about
the importance of freedom,

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.860
that begins to talk to
like-minded countries

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:22.140
about values like democracy and so on,

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:24.580
but also makes its
government more efficient,

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:29.490
then that is a much more competitive power

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:31.240
than what China has faced thus far.

